cards 393 dup ids [] dup fronts []
keyset 81 ('back', 'front', 'id', 'lesson')
keyset 235 ('back', 'front', 'grade', 'id', 'lesson')
keyset 77 ('back', 'basis', 'front', 'grade', 'id', 'lesson')
odd grades [] Counter({'T': 235, None: 81, 'C': 77})
slice-lesson cards with no basis key but grade C []
{"id": "M2-c006", "lesson": "L01", "front": "In Lab 1, what is the least N with |a_n - 2| < 0.001 for every n from N to 5000?", "back": "N = 1000. The error at n = 999 is 1.000000e-03, so the strict inequality fails there, and the error at n = 1000 is 9.990010e-04.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, a finite search over 1 to 5000; the identity |a_n - 2| = 1/(n+1) in Example 2.3"}
{"id": "M2-c013", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Does Definition 2.1 produce a limit, or only check a proposed one?", "back": "It only checks a proposed L. It does not produce L. A method is to compute a candidate and then prove it, as Example 2.3 does with the candidate 2 (Section 5)."}
{"id": "M2-c044", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Does f(x) = |x| have a derivative at 0, although it is given by one formula?", "back": "No. Q(h) = |h|/h equals 1 for h > 0 and -1 for h < 0, so the two-sided limit does not exist (Example 3.1). A formula does not guarantee a derivative at every point (Misconception 1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c064", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Is Assumption 2.1 (extreme value theorem) proved?", "back": "No. It is assumed: a function continuous at each point of [a, b] with a < b attains a maximum and a minimum on [a, b]. No grade is given. In Lesson 6, every result that uses it begins its statement with \"Assuming Assumption 2.1\"."}
{"id": "M2-c394", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What does it mean for f to be Lipschitz with constant M?", "back": "For a fixed constant M, f is Lipschitz with constant M when |f(x) - f(y)| <= M|x - y| for all x and y. The name is given in prose in Problem R3."}
{"id": "M2-c414", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is a critical point of a differentiable function (Section 1)?", "back": "A point c with f'(c) = 0 (Section 1)."}
{"id": "M2-c424", "lesson": "L01", "front": "What is a convergent sequence, and what is a divergent one?", "back": "A sequence with a limit in the sense of Definition 2.1 is convergent. A sequence with no limit is divergent (Definition 2.1)."}
